(a) The city council may authorize upon request in specific cases such variance from the terms of these regulations as will not be contrary to the public interest where, owing to special conditions, a literal enforcement of provisions of these regulations will, in an individual case, result in unnecessary hardship, so that the spirit of these regulations shall be observed, public safety and welfare secured, and substantial justice done. Provided, however, that a variance shall not be granted for a use of a sign that is prohibited by this chapter in the district in question. Such variance may be granted in an individual case upon finding by the city council that all of the following exists:
(1) There are extraordinary and exceptional conditions pertaining to the particular piece of property in question because of its size, shape, or topography;
(2) The application of these regulations to this particular piece of property would create an unnecessary hardship;
(3) Such conditions are peculiar to the particular piece of property involved;
(4) Relief, if granted, would not cause substantial detriment to the public good or impair the purposes and intent of these regulations; and
(5) The city council may impose or require such additional restrictions and standards as may be necessary to protect the health and safety of residents and workers in the community, and to protect the value and use of property in the general neighborhood.
(b) Request for variances and procedures for hearing shall be subject to the same procedures as requests for zoning amendments. The city council shall consider such request for a variance at a public meeting conducted within 35 days of the filing of a request.
(c) Minor variance. When an applicant requests a variance for any numeric criteria required under this chapter by 20 percent or less, it shall be considered a minor variance. The planning commission shall be authorized to grant such minor variance upon a finding that the criteria listed in subsection (a) of this section are met. In such cases, the procedure required by subsection (b) of this section shall not apply. Instead, the applicant shall file an application with the city for a minor variance, and shall provide notice by certified mail addressed to the owners of record of each property adjacent to the property for which the minor variance is requested. Such notice shall include a copy of the application and the date, time and location of the public meeting at which the planning commission shall consider such request. The planning commission shall consider such variance and made its decision within 35 days of the request for a variance. Such notice shall be mailed not less than 21 days prior to the date of the public meeting, and proof of the mailing of such notice shall be filed with the city prior to the meeting of the planning commission. At such meeting, the applicant and any interested parties will be allowed a reasonable opportunity to address the planning commission prior to its rendering a decision. In the event any party is aggrieved by the decision of the planning commission, the decision may be appealed to the county superior court within 30 days of the planning commission's decision.
(d) Fee. Each application for a variance shall pay a base fee as indicated on the master fee schedule to defray administrative and notification costs. Additional fees may be required as indicated on fee schedule set by the city council.
(Ord. of 12-2-2014, § 1.10)

